    "answer": "E",
    "explanation": "(A) 正確\nIn patients with recurrent or metastatic (R/M) ACC who\nnecessitate nonurgent systemic therapy, our approach is to first\nconsider their molecular classification.\nDeVita P.346\n(B) (C) 正確\nSimilar to R/M ACC, decisions regarding systemic therapy in\nnonACC should be made after reviewing tumor molecular or\ngenomic testing results. In patients with a histologic diagnosis of\nmammary analog secretory carcinoma, diagnostic testing to\nevaluate for the neurotropic tyrosine receptor kinase (NTRK) gene\nfusion is critical. Oral selective NTRK inhibitors (larotrectinib and\nentrectinib) are approved in the first line and show high response\nrates in molecularly selected patients.47 In other non-ACC\nhistologic subtypes, molecular profiling should be performed in\nconcert with AR and HER-2/neu testing by immunohistochemistry\nDeVita P.346\n(D) 正確\n\nThe 2021 ATA guidelines86 stress the importance of rapid BRAF-\nV600E assessment, because if ATC is deemed nonresectable,\n\nthen BRAF-V600E mutation positivity can guide treatment with\ncombination of dabrafenib (BRAF inhibitor) and trametinib (MEK\ninhibitor). Other agents based on nonresectable ATC molecular\nstatus include those targeting ALK fusions (crizotinib, ceritinib,\nalectinib), RET (pralsetinib, selpercatinib), and NTRK\n(larotrectinib, entrectinib).\nDeVita P.1026\n(E) 主要治療在後線可用,前線為 RAI、TKIs\nRecently, targeted therapies to potentially “reverse” the loss of\nRAI avidity in advanced DTC are particularly appealing. The\nMEK1/MEK2 inhibitor selumetinib has been shown to induce\niodine incorporation into previously RAI-R tumors, allowing a\ntherapeutic RAI dose to be delivered to the metastatic lesion.79\nThe selective BRAF inhibitor dabrafenib also stimulated RAI\nuptake in 60% of patients with metastatic BRAF-V600E–mutant\nRAI-R PTC.80 A case series at MD Anderson has demonstrated\nthis in clinical practice.81 Since mTOR (mammalian target of\nrapamycin) upregulation has been reported to be involved in the\npathogenesis of DTC, a phase II clinical trial studied the mTOR\ninhibitor everolimus for advanced RAI-R DTC, and 65% of patients\nshowed stable disease, of whom 58% showed stable disease for\n>24 weeks.82 Selpercatinib, an oral agent that selectively targets\nRET kinase, was recently FDA approved for RET fusion-positive\nnon-MTC based on objective response in 79% of patients\nDeVita P.1025",
