    "stem": "Which statement is wrong for cancer pain management?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Opioid rotation should be avoided in inadequate pain control.",
      "B": "NSAID could combine with opioid for pain control.",
      "C": "Oral form opioid has better pain control than intravenous form.",
      "D": "Short acting opioid is choice for break through pain.",
      "E": "Morphine dosage increase based on previous 3 days total opioid dosage calculation."
    },
    "sourcePage": null,
    "cancer": "Other",
    "topic": "Other",
    "trials": [],
    "answer": "A",
    "explanation": "[opioid rotation] Consider opioid rotation if pain is inadequately\ncontrolled and further dose titration is limited by adverse effects.\nOther indications for switching to a different opioid include: out-of-pocket\ncosts, limitations based on insurance formularies, or change in a\npatient’s condition (eg, dysphagia, NPO [nothing by mouth] status,\ninitiation of tube feeding, renal and/or hepatic function). (ref. NCCN\nV2.2023 PAIN-G)\n[pain crisis] short-acting PO/IV/SC 都可以使用,口服或針劑看個地區習\n慣差異(ref. 下圖 NCCN 註解 i),但如果是需要立即見效就是針劑才符合\n預期。選項C有爭議\n同意。口服嗎啡是首選的原因是因為安全考量,而不是即效性或疼痛控制\n效果。可以參考 ref. 2024 V2 MS-14: Oral administration is the preferred\nroute. An initial oral dose of 5 to 15 mg of oral short-acting morphine\nsulfate or equivalent is recommended for patients who are opioid-naïve.\nPatients presenting with severe pain needing urgent relief should be\ntreated with parenteral opioids, usually administered by the IV route or\nthe subcutaneous (SC) route. If given parenterally, the equivalent dose\nis one third of the oral dose. An initial dose of 2 to 5 mg of IV morphine\nsulfate or equivalent is recommended for patients who are opioid-naïve\n\n[D] ref. 2024 V2, MS-8: In order to achieve adequate analgesia, an initial dose\nof short-acting opioid should be determined and administered in patients who\nare\nexperiencing severe pain (or uncontrolled pain when goals of pain\nmanagement and function are not met).\n55 For patients who are opioidnaïve, this dose should be 5 to 15 mg oral or 2 to\n5 mg IV morphine sulfate or equivalent. A subcutaneous route of\nadministration can be substituted for IV; however, the time to peak effect is\ngenerally longer (~30\nminutes)\n[E] 是以 24 小時前的 total opioid dose 當作調整依據,但此題A應該更錯\n[補充 opioid reduction]\nRef. V2 2024 PAIN-G: Allow rescue doses of short-acting opioids (10%–\n20% of the 24-hour total of long-acting or regularly scheduled PO opioid\ndose) up to every 3–4 hours as needed. Titrate rescue dose as needed",
